    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Dial version listed at 1,000 watts, up to 400°F",
      "Two dials and auto shut-off: almost nothing to learn",
      "Digital version adds three savable presets and a 60-minute timer",
      "Basket and tray listed as dishwasher safe",
      "Sold in several colors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Dial model timer stops at 30 minutes",
      "Dash recommends hand washing to protect the nonstick coating",
      "One-to-two-person capacity"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An inexpensive way to find out whether an air fryer fits your routine before buying a bigger one.",
    "description": "The Dash Tasti-Crisp is the budget compact on this page: a 2.6-quart basket with simple dial controls. Dash&#39;s Amazon listings rate the dial version at 1,000 watts with a maximum of 400°F and a timer up to 30 minutes, and there is a digital version with three savable presets and a 60-minute timer if you prefer buttons. The basket and tray are nonstick and listed as dishwasher safe, though Dash&#39;s own listing recommends a quick hand wash to keep the coating in good shape. That advice is worth following on any coated basket. The appeal is how little there is to learn. You turn one dial for temperature and one for time, and the unit shuts itself off at the end. There is no app, no preset list and no adapter. Its footprint is close to the COSORI&#39;s, and it comes in several colors. The trade-offs are a coated cooking surface, a 30-minute limit on the dial model that rules out longer roasts, and a capacity that suits one or two people. It also lacks the Crispi&#39;s glass containers and storage lids. Buy it if you want an inexpensive way to see whether an air fryer fits your routine. If it does and you later want to move away from nonstick, the Crispi or the Fritaire is the natural next step."
  }
];

