          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Buy It</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The Ninja Crispi is the most interesting air fryer design in years, and it is not the right air fryer for most families. It swaps the coated drawer for glass containers that double as leftovers dishes, which is a real improvement for one to three people. It is too small to be the only cooker in a busy kitchen of five.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Ninja Crispi Is: A Heating Pod on a Glass Container
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every conventional basket air fryer is one sealed box: the heater and fan in the lid, a coated drawer underneath, and food you cannot see until you pull the drawer out. The Ninja Crispi splits that box in two. The heater and fan live in a detachable unit Ninja calls the PowerPod, and the cooking vessel is a clear borosilicate glass container that the PowerPod sits on top of. When the food is done you lift the pod off, and the container is now a serving dish or, with its lid on, a storage container.
          </p>

          <p>
            That design is the whole reason to consider the Crispi. It is still an air fryer in every way that matters for cooking: a fan pushes hot air over the food at high speed, the outside browns and the inside heats through. If you are new to the category, our explainer on <a href="/air-fryer-pros-and-cons/">air fryer pros and cons</a> covers what any air fryer does well and badly before you get to this specific model.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The PowerPod</h3>

          <p>
            The PowerPod holds the controls, the heating element and the fan, and it has heat-safe feet so you can set it down on the counter when you lift it off a container. Ninja is clear that it is designed only for its own glass containers. The manual warns never to use the PowerPod on any other vessel, so you cannot sit it on a casserole dish or a mixing bowl and treat it as a universal heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The TempWare Glass Containers</h3>

          <p>
            Ninja calls the containers TempWare (some listings say CleanCrisp glass). The standard US kit pairs a 4-quart container for full meals with a 6-cup container for a single portion or a side. Each sits on a crisper plate that lifts the food off the bottom so air can reach underneath. The large container needs an adapter to seat the PowerPod; the small one does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Comes in the Box</h3>

          <p>
            Ninja and retailer listings for the US FN101 describe the PowerPod, the 4-quart and 6-cup glass containers, two storage lids, two crisper plates and the container adapter. Ninja also sells Crispi bundles with an extra 2.5-quart container and a five-function version through some retailers, so check the exact model number and contents on the listing before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ninja Crispi Specs: What Ninja Lists for the US Model
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Crispi is sold in several regions with different specs, which causes most of the confusion in online reviews. The numbers below are for the US FN101 as Ninja and its US retail listings describe it. UK, Australian and Singapore versions use a 1,700-watt pod and metric container sizes, so ignore specs quoted from those markets if you are buying in the US.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power and Temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Ninja lists the US PowerPod at 1,500 watts on a standard 120-volt outlet, with air up to 450°F. That top temperature is higher than many compact basket models, which usually stop at 400°F, and it is where the Max Crisp function lives. Fifteen hundred watts is typical for a 4-to-6-quart air fryer and fine on an ordinary kitchen circuit, as long as you do not share that circuit with a kettle or a toaster at the same moment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            The large container is 4 quarts and the small one is 6 cups (1.5 quarts). Ninja says the 4-quart container fits up to 4 pounds of wings or 2 pounds of fries. Treat those as maximums for a full container; crisp results come from a single, loose layer, which in practice is less than the headline figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Functions</h3>

          <p>
