          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ninja Crispi Pros: What the Glass Design Gets Right
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Crispi&#39;s advantages all come from separating the heater from the vessel. None of them are about cooking food better than a good basket air fryer; they are about what happens before and after cooking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cook, Serve and Store in One Dish</h3>

          <p>
            This is the feature people buy it for. You air fry chicken thighs in the 4-quart container, lift the pod off, serve from the container and put a lid on the leftovers. The next day the same container can go in the microwave or back under the PowerPod on Recrisp. Fewer dishes, no transferring greasy food between containers, and leftovers that reheat crisp instead of soggy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Coating on the Cooking Surface</h3>

          <p>
            The container is glass rather than a coated metal drawer. Nonstick coatings on air fryer baskets are the part that wears out first, and once a coating starts to flake most owners replace the whole appliance. Glass does not have a coating to lose. Ninja markets the Crispi on that point, and for many buyers it is the deciding factor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Can See the Food</h3>

          <p>
            With clear walls you can watch fries brown without pulling a drawer and letting the heat out. That makes it easier to learn timings in the first few weeks, which is the steepest part of the air fryer learning curve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Ninja Crispi Cons: Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same design that makes the Crispi appealing also creates its limits. Most of them come down to size and to the fact that the glass is less forgiving than a metal drawer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four Quarts Is a Small-Household Size</h3>

          <p>
            The large container is 4 quarts. That is enough for two to three people, but a family of four or more will cook in batches for anything other than a side dish. If you need one appliance to feed a family every night, a 6-to-8-quart basket or a dual-basket model from our guide to the <a href="/best-air-fryer/">best air fryers</a> is a better buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glass Needs More Care</h3>

          <p>
            Borosilicate glass resists sudden temperature changes far better than ordinary glass, but it is not indestructible. Ninja&#39;s guidance is to let the containers cool before they meet cold water, a sink or a dishwasher, and the manual rules out the oven and stovetop. A dropped container can break, which a metal drawer will not. Replacement containers are sold separately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The PowerPod Is Locked to Ninja Containers</h3>

          <p>
            You cannot use the pod on any other dish. If you lose or break the containers, you are buying Ninja replacements, and you cannot expand capacity with a bigger casserole you already own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is the Ninja Crispi Big Enough? Capacity by Household
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quart ratings on air fryers describe the total volume of the container, not how much food crisps well in it. Air needs to move around each piece, so the useful load is a single layer with a little space between pieces. Use the guide below rather than the box figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One Person</h3>

          <p>
            The Crispi is generous for one. The 6-cup container handles most single meals, and the 4-quart container covers batch cooking for the week, which is exactly where the storage lids pay off. A 2-quart mini such as the Instant Vortex Mini or the COSORI Lite also works for one if you never meal prep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two to Three People</h3>

          <p>
            This is the Crispi&#39;s sweet spot. A pound of chicken pieces or a full bag of frozen fries fits the 4-quart container in one go, and you can run the small container for a side afterwards. For a deeper look at sizing, see our capacity section in the <a href="/best-air-fryer/">best air fryers</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four or More People</h3>

          <p>
            You will cook mains in two rounds, and the second round waits while the first goes cold. A family of four is better served by a large single basket, a dual-basket unit that cooks two foods at once, or an oven-style air fryer that takes a sheet pan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="glass-vs-nonstick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Glass vs Nonstick Basket: Does the Material Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cooking result depends mostly on airflow, temperature and how full the vessel is. The material matters more for durability, cleaning and what you are comfortable cooking on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nonstick Baskets</h3>

          <p>
            Most basket air fryers, including three of the alternatives on this page, use a nonstick coating. When new, food releases easily and the basket wipes clean. The coating wears over time, faster with metal utensils, abrasive sponges and harsh dishwasher detergent. Hand washing with a soft sponge is the single best way to make one last.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glass Containers</h3>

          <p>
            Glass has no coating to wear and does not react with acidic food. It is heavier, it can break, and some foods stick more than they would on a fresh nonstick surface, which is why the crisper plate matters. A short soak loosens most residue.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceramic and Stainless Alternatives</h3>

          <p>
            If you want to avoid traditional nonstick but prefer a basket, some makers now offer ceramic-coated or stainless steel baskets. Ceramic coatings are slick when new but also wear; stainless lasts but sticks more. The same trade-off applies to pans; our guide to <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">induction cookware</a> covers it in more depth for the stovetop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Clean the Ninja Crispi and Care for the Glass
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning is one of the Crispi&#39;s strengths, as long as you respect the glass. These steps follow Ninja&#39;s care guidance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Let Everything Cool</h3>

          <p>
            Lift the PowerPod off and set it on its feet. Leave the container to cool before it goes near cold water. Sudden temperature changes are the main risk to any glass cookware.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Wash the Containers, Lids and Plates</h3>

          <p>
            The glass containers and lids are dishwasher safe; Ninja&#39;s guidance places lids on the top rack. Baked-on grease comes off more easily after a short soak in warm, soapy water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Wipe the PowerPod</h3>

          <p>
            Never submerge the PowerPod or put it in the dishwasher. Once it is cool, wipe the outside with a damp cloth. Check the underside for splatter, since grease that builds up there can smoke the next time you cook.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy the Ninja Crispi (and Who Should Not)
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Crispi is a good product with a specific audience. Whether it suits you depends far more on how you cook than on any single spec.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy It If</h3>

          <p>
            You cook for one to three people, you batch cook or bring lunches, and you want to stop replacing worn nonstick baskets. The cook-serve-store workflow genuinely saves dishes, and the 450°F top temperature is generous for a compact unit. It also suits people who like to watch food cook while they learn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip It If</h3>

          <p>
            You feed four or more people most nights, you want one appliance to replace your oven for weeknight dinners, or you are hard on equipment. In those cases a larger basket or oven-style model is the better spend. If you mainly want a rice-and-steam companion for small meals rather than crisping, a <a href="/best-rice-cooker/">rice cooker</a> may earn its counter space more often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consider an Alternative If</h3>

          <p>
            You want a rotisserie or a bigger glass vessel (the Fritaire), the smallest possible footprint (the Instant Vortex Mini or COSORI Lite), or a low-cost trial run before committing (the Dash Tasti-Crisp).
          </p>
        </div>

