                Borosilicate glass tolerates temperature swings better than ordinary glass, but Ninja still says to let containers cool before they meet water or a dishwasher. A hot container in a sink of cold water is the easiest way to crack one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Containers in the Oven</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The containers are listed as microwave, freezer and dishwasher safe, but Ninja&#39;s manual says they are not oven or stovetop safe. Treat them as Crispi cookware and food storage, not as general bakeware.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting Specs From Another Region</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UK, Australian and Singapore Crispi models use a 1,700-watt pod and different container sizes and functions. If you are buying in the US, read the FN101 listing you are actually buying from rather than a review written for another market.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling the Container</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 4-pound wing figure is a maximum, not a recipe. Piled food steams instead of crisping. Spread food in one loose layer on the crisper plate and cook a second round if needed; the result is better and often no slower.
              </p>
